rategy.pdf","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","date":"18/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"5. ward budget","url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s215936/5-ward-budget.pdf","committee":"Castle Ward Committee - Expired","date":"17/03/2026","key":false},{"title":"3. Q3 Outturn 2025-26","url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s215857/3-q3-outturn-2025-26.pdf","committee":"Cabinet","date":"16/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"7. Financial Statement","url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s215658/7-financial-statement.pdf","committee":"Fawdon and West Gosforth Ward Committee - Expired","date":"12/03/2026","key":false},{"title":"Appendix 12 - 2026-27 Treasury Management Strategy","url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s215420/appendix-12-2026-27-treasury-management-strategy.pdf","committee":"City Council","date":"04/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"Appendix 13 Minimum Revenue Provision Policy Statement","url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s215421/appendix-13-minimum-revenue-provision-policy-statement.pdf","committee":"City Council","date":"04/03/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"City Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-08","committee":"Corporate Scrutiny Committee","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Place Scrutiny Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-15","committee":"Health and Social Care Scrutiny Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Standing Advisory Council on Religious Education","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Children, Families and Communities Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Provisional","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Place Scrutiny Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"North East Museums Finance, Audit and Risk Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Children, Families and Communities Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Provisional","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Health and Social Care Scrutiny Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Newcastle council's uncollected prior-year council tax jumps 58% in a year to \u00a316.87m","lead":"Newcastle upon Tyne has seen uncollected council tax from previous years surge by more than half in twelve months, growing from \u00a310.69m to \u00a316.87m in the first quarter of 2026, the council's Cabinet papers show. The increase signals a significant deterioration in the council's ability to collect tax arrears despite current-year collection tracking on target at 28.1%. Prior-year business rates arrears have also grown, rising 38% to \u00a37.87m. The papers do not explain the cause of the arrears rise or detail recovery plans. The growth coincides with the council projecting a \u00a36.7m overspend in the first quarter against a \u00a3340m total budget, driven largely by demand-led services including adult social care and community support.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"6. Council Tax Support Cabinet","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219034/219034.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Newcastle's housing complaint handling breaches statutory code as Ombudsman finds systemic failures","lead":"Newcastle's housing complaint handling does not comply with the statutory Complaints Handling Code, the council acknowledged through its regulatory action plan. The Housing Ombudsman identified severe mismanagement and systemic failures.\n\nThe Ombudsman issued eight determinations in 2025-26: one severe mismanagement, six mismanagement, one service failure. The Ombudsman found delays, damp and mould, repeat repairs and weak oversight of complex repair work. Repairs account for more than half of all housing complaints.\n\nThe council received 1,062 housing complaints in 2025-26, more than double the 497 in 2024-25. Response times fall short of target: 67.9% of initial complaints and 32.6% of escalated complaints were answered within timescale. The council plans faster responses in 2026-27. The Ombudsman can issue a Complaint Handling Failure Notice if standards are not met.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"6. Council Tax Support Cabinet","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219034/219034.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Newcastle housing service found to have severe maladministration and systemic failures","lead":"Newcastle City Council's housing service has been found to have severe maladministration and systemic failures in repairs, complaint handling and record-keeping, according to eight determinations issued by the Housing Ombudsman in 2025\u201326. The independent regulator identified one case of severe maladministration and six of maladministration. The council is not meeting statutory timescales for responding to complaints: only 32.6 per cent of escalated complaints were answered within the required deadline, against a target of 100 per cent. Repairs and construction, which account for more than half of all housing complaints, were identified as a particular problem area, with delays, damp and mould, repeat issues and weak oversight of complex repairs. The Ombudsman found that investigations did not address every point raised by residents and that limited record-keeping weakened evidence of decisions and actions. The council has acknowledged it is not fully compliant with the Housing Ombudsman's statutory Complaints Handling Code and faces the risk of enforcement action.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"6. Council Tax Support Cabinet","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219034/219034.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","headline":"Municipal waste landfilled increased sharply to 11.9% from 3.4%\u2014a 249% deterioration\u2014without documented explanation.","detail":"The proportion of municipal waste sent to landfill rose significantly from 3.4% (Q3 2025-26) to 11.9% (Q4 2025-26), reversing positive performance. The report marks this as a direction of concern ('Increasing'), provides no explanation, and notes comparator data is 'not available.' This could indicate capital scheme failure (processing or recycling equipment breakdown), supply chain disruption, permitting issues, or a shift toward lower-cost disposal.","category":"other","matters":"An unexplained spike in landfill disposal may signal operational failure, uncontrolled cost inflation, or capital scheme problems affecting other council services. The absence of explanation for a 249% increase is itself a governance risk signal suggesting either data quality issues or management attention gaps.","figures":"11.9% (Q4 2025-26) versus 3.4% (Q3 2025-26)","quote":"Amount of Waste NI191 - Residual municipal waste landfilled (lower is better) 11.9% (Q4 2025-26) 3.4% (Q3 2025-26) Increasing","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","headline":"Council tax and sundry debt collection rates have improved in Q1 2026-27, meeting or exceeding targets and reducing collection risk.","detail":"Sundry debt collection reached 90.4% within 90 days, exceeding the 90.0% target and improving from 89.3%. Council tax in-year collection is 28.1% at end June\u20140.6 percentage points above the prior year\u2014placing the council on track for the 97.0% year-end target. The council attributes council tax improvement to amendments to the student exemptions process following the Renters' Rights Act.","category":"income","matters":"Improved collection rates reduce bad debt risk, strengthen cash flow available to service borrowing, and support financial planning certainty. This partially offsets concerns about HRA rent collection decline and demonstrates management focus on revenue collection.","figures":"Sundry debt 90.4% versus target 90.0% (up from 89.3%); Council tax 28.1% at end June 2026 versus 27.5% at end June 2025","quote":"Council Tax in-year collection rate (higher is better) 28.1% 27.5% (June 2025) Increasing / better than target Year-end target 97.0%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","headline":"A new transformation programme has been flagged as an amber-priority financial sustainability risk, adding to existing red-rated financial resilience concerns.","detail":"The 'Change and Innovation Newcastle Programme' is registered as a new amber-priority risk (priority 9), with the risk assessment noting the programme may fail to 'achieve its intended cross-council transformational impact on customer experience, service effectiveness and long-term financial sustainability.' This new risk sits alongside the council's existing red-rated strategic risk on financial sustainability, indicating transformation is viewed as necessary to achieve long-term viability.","category":"governance","matters":"The addition of a transformation programme risk explicitly linked to financial sustainability signals the council's baseline operations may not be sustainable without significant change. Programme execution risk compounds already-elevated concerns about the council's financial position and adds delivery risk to a critical plan.","figures":"Amber risk, priority 9, newly identified","quote":"There is a risk that, without sustained stakeholder alignments, sufficient resourcing and a clear scope the Change and Innovation Newcastle Programme may not achieve its intended cross-council transformational impact on customer experience, service effectiveness and long-term financial sustainability.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","headline":"For the first time since 2006, Newcastle has had to place homeless households in bed and breakfast emergency accommodation.","detail":"Quarter 1 marked an end to an 18-year period without using emergency B&B placements, signalling that the council's temporary accommodation stock has been exhausted or that supply pressures have overwhelmed available provision. B&B placements are significantly more expensive than council-managed temporary housing and indicate both immediate capacity crisis and potential escalating ongoing costs.","category":"demand","matters":"Emergency B&B use signals housing system failure and statutory duty strain. It represents a cost spike and indicates the council may face escalating costs and service pressure, with implications for overall budget sustainability and potential intervention risk if demand pressures persist.","figures":"First B&B use in 18 years; previous use ended in 2006","quote":"Quarter 1 was the first quarter since 2006 when we needed to use bed and breakfast accommodation","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","headline":"Housing Revenue Account rent collection has declined to 95.87% from 99.04%, reducing income available for housing investment and debt servicing.","detail":"Quarterly rent collection in the HRA fell from 99.04% to 95.87% (reaching 98.15% by week 13). This reduces cash income to fund repairs, maintenance, and debt servicing. The decline occurs against the council's classification of housing services as a red-rated strategic risk that remains static\u2014not improving\u2014making rental income stability critical to HRA sustainability.","category":"income","matters":"HRA borrowing is typically serviced from rental income. A declining collection rate directly affects the HRA's debt servicing capacity and may constrain future capital investment in housing stock maintenance and improvement.","figures":"95.87% (98.15% at week 13) versus previous 99.04%","quote":"Total rent collected as a % of rent due (higher is better) 95.87% (98.15% at week 13) 99.04% Decreasing","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-21"},"name":"Newcastle","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Municipal waste landfilled increased sharply to 11.9% from 3.4%\u2014a 249% deterioration\u2014without documented explanation.","lead":"An unexplained spike in landfill disposal may signal operational failure, uncontrolled cost inflation, or capital scheme problems affecting other council services. The absence of explanation for a 249% increase is itself a governance risk signal suggesting\u2026","also":["25 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Standards Committee on 2026-09-29","n_findings":26,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"8.1 Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Q1 Council Performance report","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.newcastle.gov.uk/documents/s219040/219040.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":125,"news_drafts_total":3}}